meta {
  name: 01 - Direct RLS: user A reading user B's transactions via PostgREST
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

get {
  url: {{supabaseUrl}}/rest/v1/transactions?user_id=eq.{{user_id_b}}&select=*
  body: none
  auth: none
}

headers {
  apikey: {{supabaseAnonKey}}
  Authorization: Bearer {{access_token}}
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
}

tests {
  test("PostgREST returns 200, not 401/403", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });
  test("row-level security silently filters out user B's transactions", function () {
    expect(res.getBody()).to.be.an("array").that.is.empty;
  });
}

docs {
  Bypasses the NestJS backend entirely -- hits Supabase PostgREST
  directly with user A's real JWT and the anon key (the backend never
  uses service_role either, see supabase-auth.guard.ts, so this is the
  exact same access path the backend itself uses, not a synthetic
  worst case). Proves transactions_select_own (001_init_schema.sql)
  does the isolating on its own, independent of anything the NestJS
  layer might add on top.

  Run order: 00 Sign In (captures access_token for user A), then
  Sign In User B (captures user_id_b), then this request.

  Expected: HTTP 200 with an EMPTY array -- not 401/403. RLS doesn't
  reject the request, it silently removes rows the caller can't see.
  Companion: 04-direct-rls-own-user-visible, same shape filtered to
  user A's own id, needed to prove this path isn't just denying
  everyone.
}
